before:
```react
function useToggle(initial) {
  const [enabled, setEnabled] = React.useState(initial);
  return [enabled, () => setEnabled(value => !value)];
}
function App() {
  const [enabled, toggle] = useToggle(false);
  return <button onClick={toggle}>{enabled ? "On" : "Off"}</button>;
}
```

Each call gets its own state. Sharing a hook definition shares logic, not one shared stored value.

Change only the initial argument to true:
```react
function useToggle(initial) {
  const [enabled, setEnabled] = React.useState(initial);
  return [enabled, () => setEnabled(value => !value)];
}
function App() {
  const [enabled, toggle] = useToggle(true);
  return <button onClick={toggle}>{enabled ? "On" : "Off"}</button>;
}
```
This instance starts On; one click shows Off. useToggle(initial) returns the current Boolean and an action function. React invokes that function on the click; it queues the opposite pending value. The initial argument seeds useState on mount, not on every later render. In the separate useCounter exercise, reset explicitly uses the initial argument from the current render when that action is invoked; initialization and an explicit reset are different operations.

after:
```react
function useToggle(initial) {
  const [enabled, setEnabled] = React.useState(initial);
  return [enabled, () => setEnabled(value => !value)];
}
function App() {
  const [enabled, toggle] = useToggle(false);
  return <button onClick={toggle}>{enabled ? "On" : "Off"}</button>;
}
```
The button starts Off. One click makes it On. `useToggle` returns the current value and an action that changes it.
